==English==
{{wikipedia}}

{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|literal|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{term|litteralis|lang=la}}, also {{term|literalis|lang=la||of or pertaining to letters or to writing}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|littera|lang=en}}, {{term|litera||a letter}}; see [[letter]].

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Exactly as [[state]]d; [[read]] or [[understand|understood]] without additional [[interpretation]]; [[accord]]ing to the [[letter]] or verbal expression; [[real]]; not [[figurative]] or [[metaphorical]].
#: ''The '''literal''' translation is “hands full of bananas” but it means empty-handed.''
# [[follow|Following]] the letter or exact words; not [[free]]; not taking [[liberties]].
#: ''A '''literal''' reading of the law would prohibit it, but that is clearly not the intent.''
# {{context|uncommon}} Consisting of, or expressed by, [[letter]]s.

